      'Regular property checks with reports, so you know the place is right between stays, not just on changeover day.',
      'Trades arranged. If something needs fixing, you hear it from us first, and we organise a licensed tradie.',
      "One contact for the whole property, so there's one local number to ring instead of a cleaner, a handyman and a key box.",
    ],
  },
];

// §5 "Worth knowing" — the interruption block. Callout, tone="note", the
// same job it does on the NDIS page: correct something the reader probably
// believes before they compare on price.
//
// The lever here is the BFD's own consequence framing for this audience:
// what inaction costs a host is not a dirty house, it is the review. That is
// the one argument a cheaper quote cannot answer.
const worthKnowing = {
  heading: 'A turnover is priced per clean. It is judged per review.',
  body: [
    'A turnover is the only clean on this site whose result gets published. A missed shower screen is a private annoyance in a family home and a permanent three-star sentence on a listing, and it lands on the review that sits at the top of your page for the next six months.',
    'So the number worth comparing is not the price per turnover. It is what happens when a guest checks out at eleven and the next one arrives at two, and whether anybody is going to tell you the toaster is broken before a guest does.',
  ],
};

// §6 Why the same team matters. TextBlock dark, the sibling pages' "worth
// knowing" long-form slot.
//
// This is where the BFD's "first month is the critical proving period" and
// "once a cleaner proves dependable, hosts rarely switch" insight earns its
// place — stated as what the host should demand, not as what TLB claims.
const whySameTeamBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Short-stay cleaning has the highest turnover of cleaners of any work we do, and it is not because the job is hard. It is because it is scheduled around other people, it clusters on Fridays and Sundays, and a sole operator with six listings physically cannot cover a Saturday when four of them change over at once. So the good ones get busy, then they get overcommitted, then one weekend they do not show, and it is your guest standing on the verandah, not theirs.',
  'That is the whole reason TLB is a team rather than one person with a car. Capacity is what makes a Saturday changeover boring, and boring is the entire product. When you are choosing between quotes, the questions worth asking are how many people the business actually employs, what happens when your regular cleaner is sick, and who answers the phone at nine on a Sunday morning.',
  'The person in your property is on our team, we know which property is yours, and there is one local number to ring, which is a low bar until the weekend you need it.',
];

// §7 Before/after. Shared with five other pages, per the
// same-structure-everywhere call.
//
// These frames are REAL TLB JOB PHOTOGRAPHS, published with the client's
// permission and with nothing identifying in frame — no house numbers, street
// signs, mail, people, or names on doors. That is why this section carries no
// "illustrative samples" note. Do NOT wire the `sample-` files back in without
// restoring a note that says so: a fabricated "before and after from a real
// job" is a false trust claim, not filler.
//
// ⚠️ BOTH PAIRS ARE KITCHEN SURFACES. The shower/bathroom pair this section
// used to carry was dropped rather than shown as an illustration next to a
// photograph, so nothing on this page evidences bathroom work. A real shower
// pair is the outstanding shoot; when it lands it takes slot 2 back.
//
// THE PHOTO BRIEF this stands in for, and it is a better brief on this page
// than on any other: a turnover is the one clean where before and after are
// twenty minutes apart, in a property nobody lives in, with no tenant or
// client to consent — so it is by far the easiest place on the site to shoot
// a real pair. One fixed tripod position, nothing moved between frames.
// Kitchen bench and shower screen are the two that read at a glance.
//
// ⚠️ Written permission from the host is still required, and no identifying
// detail in frame — no street numbers, no mail, no guest belongings.
const beforeAfterPairs: BeforeAfterPair[] = [
  {
    before: {
      src: kitchenBenchtopBeforeImg,
      alt: 'A kitchen benchtop before a TLB turnover clean, with dishes stacked in and beside the sink and pots left on the stove',
    },
    after: {
      src: kitchenBenchtopAfterImg,
      alt: 'The same kitchen benchtop after the turnover, sink empty and the bench clear and wiped down',
    },
    caption: 'Kitchen benchtop and sink',
  },
  {
    before: {
      src: cooktopBeforeImg,
      alt: 'A gas cooktop before a TLB turnover clean, with spilled coffee grounds, crumbs and food scraps across the black enamel',
    },
    after: {
      src: cooktopAfterImg,
      alt: 'The same gas cooktop after the turnover, the enamel wiped back to an even black finish',
    },
    caption: 'Gas cooktop and burners',
  },
];

// §8 What we turn over. The property types this work actually covers, as
// pills — the same shape the commercial pages use for premises types.
//
// ⚠️ Every href is a flat kebab-case guess from its label except the four
// that already exist elsewhere in the site (deep, end of lease, mould,
// house cleaning). Unconfirmed sitewide, per navigation.ts.
const propertyTags = [
  { label: 'Airbnb and Stayz listings', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Whole-house holiday lets', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Beach houses and coastal cottages', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Granny flats and studios', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Apartments and units', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Hinterland retreats and cabins', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Multi-listing portfolios', href: quoteCta.href },
  { label: 'Deep clean between seasons', href: '/house-cleaning/deep-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Mould cleaning and removal', href: '/house-cleaning/mould-removal/' },
  { label: 'End of lease and bond cleans', href: '/house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/' },
];

// §9 Social proof. Bracketed [TBC] briefing slots, NOT real testimonials.
// Never invent a quote, a host, or a property. BFD 5.3 requires written
// permission before naming a client at all, and IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 forbids
// generating a face for one — the avatars stay as labelled placeholders.
const testimonials = [
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from a host about a same-day changeover that landed on time, ideally a Saturday with a tight window. That is the single most valuable testimonial this page can carry, because reliability on the hard day is the whole claim.]',
    name: '[Host first name, TBC]',
    role: '[Property type and town, for example "Holiday house, Suffolk Park", TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'TLB holiday let client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from an out-of-area owner, Brisbane or Sydney, about not having to be there. This is the one that reaches the remote investor, who is the highest-value reader on the page.]',
    name: '[Host first name, TBC]',
    role: '[Remote owner, town of the listing, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Out-of-area TLB holiday let client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote that mentions the restocking or the presentation specifically, and if a host has ever quoted a guest review back to her, ask whether we can use that. A guest praising the property in a host\'s own words is stronger proof than anything we can say.]',
    name: '[Host first name, TBC]',
    role: '[Property type and town, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Long-standing TLB holiday let client' },
  },
];

// §10 Comparison table. The shared approved rows, now on a fifth page.
//
// ⚠️ ONE ROW IS OFF-TARGET HERE and it is worth a decision rather than a
// shrug: "Ongoing contracts with the region's leading real estate agencies"
// answers an agency's question, not a host's. It is the row the NDIS page
// judged wrong for its audience and dropped the whole table over. Kept here
// because for a host it still reads as "this is a real business other
// professionals rely on", and because the trust bar above already makes the
// same claim on every page. If TLB would rather it were host-specific, the
// fix is a page-local `rows` array with a holiday-let row in its place —
// not an edit to the shared file, which four other pages read.
const comparisonTable = {
  heading: 'How TLB compares',
  cornerLabel: 'Why TLB Cleaning is different',
  lead: 'Anyone can clean a holiday let once. The thing worth paying for is the fiftieth changeover being as boring as the first.',
  columns: comparisonColumns,
  // The shared rows, unchanged, plus one supplied row (September 2026) for
  // this page only. Appended here rather than in comparison.ts so the other
  // pages' tables are untouched; Full Property Care exists only on this page.
  rows: [
    ...comparisonRows,
    {
      label: 'Property checks and trades',
      values: ['Varies by platform', 'Depends on the person', 'Included in Full Property Care'],
    },
  ],
};

// §11 Branded FAQs — what hosts ask us. Supplied copy (September 2026),
// verbatim: five kept, four new. The kept answers came without their
// [Confirm] brackets (calendar mechanism, same-day cut-off), so those are gone.
const brandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Do I have to tell you every time I get a booking?',
    answer:
      'No, that is the part we take off you. We work from your calendar rather than from your messages, so a new booking or a moved date flows through without you relaying it. You will still hear from us when something needs a decision.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you do a same-day turnover?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and most of our short-stay work is same-day. Checkout to check-in is usually three or four hours, which is enough for a full reset with the right number of people on it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What gets restocked, and how is it charged?',
    answer:
      'On Turnover + Restock and Full Property Care, we top up loo paper, soap, dishwashing, tea, coffee and kitchen staples, and set up the welcome touches. Supplies are itemised on your invoice, so you see exactly what was used.',
  },
  {
    question: "I'm not there. How do I know it was done?",
    answer:
      "You get photos after every turnover, the same day, plus anything we've flagged. On Full Property Care we also run regular property checks and send you a report, so you know the place is right between stays too.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if a guest leaves the place in a state?',
    answer:
      'We clean it and we tell you, with what we found, the same day. An extra-hours charge only ever happens after we have told you and you have said yes, so you will not find a surprise on an invoice. If it is bad enough to be a claim, you have it in writing from us while it still matters.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who pays the tradie if something breaks?',
    answer:
      "You do, and only after you've said yes. We tell you what's wrong, with a photo, and once you say yes, we send one of the licensed trades we work with. The work is itemised on your TLB invoice, so you only deal with us.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Will it be the same cleaner every time?',
    answer:
      'Wherever we can, yes, and it makes a real difference on a short-stay property, someone who knows where the spare linen lives and which door sticks is faster and misses less. When your regular cleaner is away you get someone else from our team who has been briefed on your property, not a stranger from a platform.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there a lock-in?',
    answer: "No. There's no lock-in contract, no minimum term and no exit fee.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      'We ask about the property, how often it turns over and what presentation you want, and we look at it in person if it is local. Then you get a price and a scope in writing. No obligation, no lock-in contract. If it suits, we set it up around your calendar and it starts happening.',
  },
];

// §12 Non-branded FAQs — the long-tail block, aimed at what hosts search
// rather than what they ask us. Supplied copy (September 2026), verbatim: five
// kept, one new. The kept answers came without their [Confirm] brackets
// (pricing basis, linen), so those are gone.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS STILL RECOMMENDED. Two of these make checkable claims
// about things outside TLB's control:
//   - The cleaning-fee answer describes how a fee behaves on a booking
//     platform. Deliberately platform-neutral, because the platforms change
//     their own fee mechanics without notice.
//   - The night-cap answer describes NSW short-term rental planning rules and
//     a shire day cap. See the file header: it needs a compliance read and a
//     "last reviewed" date, or it comes off the page.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How much does Airbnb cleaning cost?',
    answer:
      'Short-stay turnovers are usually priced as a flat rate per changeover rather than hourly, because a host needs to know the number before they set their cleaning fee. The rate moves with the size of the property, how many bedrooms and bathrooms turn over, whether linen is included and how tight the same-day window is. Ask for it against a written scope, not an hourly rate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is short-term rental cleaning different from Airbnb cleaning?',
    answer:
      "No, it's the same job. Short-term rental cleaning covers any short-stay listing, whether it's on Airbnb, Stayz, Booking.com or your own site. We work off whichever booking calendar you use.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Should the guest cleaning fee cover the whole turnover?',
    answer:
      "Usually it covers most of it, and hosts who try to make it cover everything tend to lose bookings. The fee is visible at checkout and a high one reads as a red flag on a short stay. The common approach is to set the fee at or a little under the real turnover cost and treat the rest as a cost of doing business, then price the nightly rate accordingly. Check your platform's current rules before you rely on any of this: how cleaning fees are displayed, refunded on cancellation and counted toward your total is set by the platform, and it changes.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Who supplies the linen and towels?',
    answer:
      'It goes one of two ways, and it is worth settling before the first clean. Either the host keeps three sets per bed on site and the cleaner turns and launders them, or a linen service delivers hired linen and takes the used sets away. Hired linen costs more per turnover and removes the laundry bottleneck, which is what usually breaks a same-day changeover.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a turnover actually take?',
    answer:
      'A one-bedroom apartment is usually an hour to ninety minutes with one person. A three-bedroom house with two bathrooms is closer to three hours, or half that with two cleaners on it, which is how a same-day changeover gets done inside a three-hour window at all. Laundry is the part that does not compress: if the linen has to be washed and dried on site between guests, that sets the floor, not the cleaning.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are there rules about how many nights I can let my place?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ COMPLIANCE READ REQUIRED — see the file header. Written without
      // dates, precinct names or a day count on purpose: every one of those
      // is exactly the kind of specific that goes stale silently and that a
      // host may act on. Do not "improve" this by adding them without a
      // verification pass and a visible last-reviewed date.
      'In NSW, short-term rental accommodation is regulated: properties generally have to be listed on the state register, meet fire-safety requirements and comply with a code of conduct, and non-hosted letting is capped at a set number of nights a year in some areas. The Byron Shire has its own reduced cap, applied unevenly across the shire, so two listings a few streets apart can sit under different limits. Queensland is different again and several Gold Coast rules are set locally. Check the current position with your council and the NSW planning portal before you rely on a number. This is general information, not planning advice, and the rules in this region have changed more than once.',
  },
];

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.
// Kicker matches the hero's, the pattern the commercial leaves set.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Airbnb and holiday let cleaning',
  heading: 'Hand us the calendar.',
  lead: 'We look at the property, ask how often it turns over and what presentation you want, and put a price and a scope in writing. No lock-in, no obligation.',
  cta: { label: 'Talk to us today', href: quoteCta.href },
};
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      subheading={hero.subheading}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid
        across both sections. 75% keeps Dark Teal body copy above 4.5:1 over
        the darkest part of the frame. Same treatment as every sibling. */}
    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={pageTrustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    <TextBlock theme="light" align="left" heading="Short-term rental and holiday home cleaning" body={shortStayBody} />

    <ServiceBlocks variant="icon-grid" heading="What we take care of" items={takeCareOfItems} />

    <PathwayCards heading={pathwayCards.heading} headingAccent={pathwayCards.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards.cards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading="What a turnover includes"
      items={turnoverItems}
      cta={{ label: 'Talk to us today', href: quoteCta.href }}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={levelsTable.heading}
      lead={levelsTable.lead}
      columns={levelsTable.columns}
      rows={levelsTable.rows}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={worthKnowing.heading} body={worthKnowing.body} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading="Why short-term rental cleaners disappear, and what to ask before you book one"
      body={whySameTeamBody}
    />

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair. The
        pairs are both real TLB job photography. See the §7 comment for the
        shower pair that is still outstanding — nothing on this page
        evidences bathroom work until it is shot. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading="The difference, on real jobs."
      lead="Drag the handle across each photo to see the same spot before and after a clean."
      pairs={beforeAfterPairs}
      note="Photographed on real TLB cleans, published with the client's permission."
    />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="image-cards"
      heading="What you get after every stay"
      lead="You don't have to take our word that it's done. After every turnover you get a short report, so you can see the place is ready before your next guest arrives."
      items={afterEveryStayItems}
      numbered
    />

    <TextBlock
      id="owners-who-live-away"
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading="Holiday home cleaning for owners who live away"
      body={ownersAwayBody}
      cta={{ label: 'Talk to us today', href: quoteCta.href }}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading="What we turn over"
      subheading="Short-stay and holiday properties of every shape, across both sides of the border."
      tags={propertyTags}
      note={["Something not on the list? Ask us anyway."]}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={comparisonTable.heading}
      cornerLabel={comparisonTable.cornerLabel}
      lead={comparisonTable.lead}
      columns={comparisonTable.columns}
      rows={comparisonTable.rows}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What our hosts say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    {/* ✅ THE FULL FOOTPRINT, per the copy-rewrite brief §21, which specifies
        this list town for town. Three reasons and they stack.

        The hero promises the Tweed and this band used to drop it, so the page
        stated two different footprints. That omission is worse here than on
        most pages: Kingscliff, Casuarina, Cabarita Beach, Pottsville and
        Hastings Point are among the densest short-stay towns in the whole
        footprint, and BFD §6.1 names "the Tweed coast" explicitly as where
        these owners are.

        `airbnb cleaning services gold coast` is worth 30/mo and the Queensland
        block named two towns out of nine. Coolangatta, Currumbin and Miami are
        all short-stay towns and were all missing.

        And it settles the geography question §0 raises about the approved meta:
        the real footprint is much wider than "Byron to Palm Beach" implies, and
        this is where the page shows it. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading="Byron Bay, Lennox Head, Ballina, Kingscliff, Cabarita Beach, Pottsville, Coolangatta, Palm Beach and Burleigh, plus the hinterland stays."
      groups={completeFootprintLocationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `instant` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        ⚠️ FLAGGED, NOT CHANGED. The house rules reserve "instant pricing" for
        regular cleaning and send everything else to "fast quoting", because BFD
        §13.2 says instant pricing works where the job is predictable and badly
        where condition drives the price. This page is neither of those things
        by the letter of the rule, and `instant` is still right: a turnover is
        the same property to the same scope every time, which is exactly what
        the third paragraph of this block argues, so it gets priced once rather
        than quoted after every checkout. The copy-rewrite brief §6 reaches the
        same conclusion and says to flag it rather than pick a side, since the
        binary in the rules does not quite fit this page. Raised for the
        workbook owner; the page's own reasoning for the exception is sound.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs.

        "How it works" steps: supplied copy (September 2026), now shared from
        brand-lines.ts with every page that carries this section. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.instant}
      body={[
        managedForYou.recurring,
        ...theLineBody.turnover,
        ...howItWorks.turnover,
      ]}
    />

    {/* ✅ BOTH FAQ HEADINGS RENAMED (copy-rewrite brief §23 and §24).

        The page carries two FAQ blocks and the generic "Frequently Asked
        Questions" on the first made the split look like a build error. The
        first block is about working with TLB, the second is about the category.

        The second heading also lands `airbnb cleaning services` contiguously,
        which is the H2 keyword component the page previously half-scored, and
        drops the Title Case that BFD §9.2 rules out. */}
    <Faq heading="Working with us on your property" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Airbnb cleaning services, common questions" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
